{"id":"n-20261025-qzi1ik","category":"travel","display_title":"Walking the New Wales Forest Trail","display_title_he":"הליכה בשביל היער החדש בוויילס","short_summary":"Explore the first pilot section of the Wales Forest Trail, a 50-mile hiking route connecting woodlands across south Wales's old mining region.","english_level":"C1","words":406,"reading_time":3,"image":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1c/Walking_Trail_Garwnant_Forest_Centre_-_geograph.org.uk_-_4611181.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"Nigel Mykura · CC BY-SA 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Walking_Trail_Garwnant_Forest_Centre_-_geograph.org.uk_-_4611181.jpg"},"sources":[{"name":"The Guardian","domain":"theguardian.com","url":"https://www.theguardian.com/travel/2026/oct/20/south-wales-new-forest-trail-old-mining-villages","title":"‘How green are our valleys?’: in the footsteps of miners on south Wales’s new forest trail","published_at":"2026-10-20T06:00:20.000Z"}],"published_at":"2026-10-20T06:00:20.000Z","first_published_in_app_at":"2026-10-25T06:13:51.933Z","quality_score":9,"interest_score":8,"verification_status":"verified","content_status":"published","paragraphs":["A new ambitious hiking route has recently emerged across the landscape of south Wales, inviting outdoor enthusiasts to explore a landscape shaped by both nature and industrial history. Known as the Wales Forest Trail, or Llwybr Coedwig Cymru Trail, this fifty-mile pilot route stretches from Porthkerry Park near Barry to Pont Walby, close to Glynneath. According to the National Forest for Wales, this path represents the initial phase of a grander vision to connect woodlands spanning the entire length and breadth of the country, acting as a sylvan counterpart to the famous Wales Coast Path.","Historically, the communities nestled in the south Wales valleys maintained a striking disconnection from the wild, elevated hills looming directly above them. While mountain bikers discovered the region's potential long ago, hikers typically gravitated toward traditional coastal paths or national parks. The new pilot trail aims to change that dynamic by guiding walkers through a diverse mosaic of environments. Starting near an old railway viaduct in Porthkerry Park, the path winds past birch and oak trees, navigates roadside pavements, and dips into tranquil sanctuaries like Hensol Forest, where ancient pine needles cushion the forest floor.","Along the journey, the route accommodates various users, including cyclists, horse riders, and pushchair users, while thoughtfully intersecting with post-industrial communities. In Blackmill, for instance, the trail passes Coed Bach Dimbath, a local community woodland featuring accessible paths, an outdoor classroom, and an otter hide built by residents. According to Natural Resources Wales project lead Rhodri Hewitt, areas that remained largely inaccessible for decades are now open, allowing people to reconnect with nearby woodlands that once felt entirely separate from daily life, even though the valleys can occasionally feel desperately poor.","As the trail continues northward into regions like Nant-y-Moel and across the dramatic plateau of Tarren y Fforch, hikers traverse paths historically trodden by miners walking to work in pits located in neighboring valleys. Beyond its industrial heritage, the route encounters striking natural landmarks, including Afan Forest Park, the highest point of the valleys at Craig y Llyn, and the rainforest-like gorge of Cwm Gwrelych, which boasts 300-million-year-old exposed strata, glossy rivers, and rushing waterfalls. Concluding near the Isambard Kingdom Brunel-designed viaduct at Pont Walby, the route embodies an old Welsh proverb mentioned by project organizers: 'Dod yn ôl at fy nghoed,' meaning to return to one's trees and find a balanced state of mind after a tiring week of walking or underground toil."],"facts":[{"fact":"A new 50-mile pilot hiking route called the Wales Forest Trail (Llwybr Coedwig Cymru Trail) has been launched, running from Porthkerry Park near Barry to Pont Walby near Glynneath.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"The trail is the first section of a planned National Forest for Wales that aims to link woodlands across the length and breadth of Wales.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Parts of the trail follow historical paths that miners once took across the hills to work in pits in neighbouring valleys.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"The pilot route incorporates various landscapes, including ancient pine forests, community woodlands like Coed Bach Dimbath, commercial forests, and geological sites of special scientific interest such as Cwm Gwrelych.","sources":[1]}],"glossary":[{"text":"new","lemma":"new","pos":"adj","he":"חדש","name":false},{"text":"ambitious","lemma":"ambitious","pos":"adj","he":"שאיפתני, שאפתני","name":false},{"text":"hiking","lemma":"hiking","pos":"noun","he":"טיול רגלי","name":false},{"text":"route","lemma":"route","pos":"noun","he":"מסלול","name":false},{"text":"recently","lemma":"recently","pos":"adv","he":"לאחרונה","name":false},{"text":"emerged","lemma":"emerge","pos":"verb","he":"הגיח, הופיע","name":false},{"text":"across","lemma":"across","pos":"prep","he":"ברחבי","name":false},{"text":"landscape","lemma":"landscape","pos":"noun","he":"נוף","name":false},{"text":"south Wales","lemma":"south Wales","pos":"noun","he":"דרום וויילס","name":false},{"text":"inviting","lemma":"invite","pos":"verb","he":"מזמין","name":false},{"text":"outdoor","lemma":"outdoor","pos":"adj","he":"חוץ","name":false},{"text":"enthusiasts","lemma":"enthusiast","pos":"noun","he":"חובבים","name":false},{"text":"explore","lemma":"explore","pos":"verb","he":"לחקור","name":false},{"text":"shaped","lemma":"shape","pos":"verb","he":"עוצב","name":false},{"text":"nature","lemma":"nature","pos":"noun","he":"טבע","name":false},{"text":"industrial","lemma":"industrial","pos":"adj","he":"תעשייתי","n